### Owner Shares Built with the Longitudinal Weight {#sec-appendix-lnw}



Our main owner shares weight each owner by the HILDA cross-sectional responding-person weight, because each share describes the owners of an industry in a single wave. As a sensitivity check, @tbl-b1-lnw re-estimates the main specification and the two decay weightings with the shares weighted instead by the longitudinal responding-person weight. The decay rates are calibrated on these shares by an earlier version of the cross-validation in @sec-loo-shares, which used a narrower employee sample and did not control for the worker's origin, giving $\lambda = 0.02$ for Type 1 and $\lambda_{\text{MES}} = 0.44$ and $\lambda_{\text{MNES}} = 0.01$ for Type 2. This weight is zero in wave 1 and positive only for people who have responded in every wave since wave 1, so it gives weight to a shrinking subset of owners. Over waves 2 to 21 it is positive for 57 per cent of owner person-years, but for only 24 per cent of the person-years of low-English owners, and for 14 of the 47 low-English owners observed in waves 1 to 21.

Without decay, the MES and high-English interactions have the same signs and similar sizes under both weights. The low-English interaction does not. Without decay it is $0.456$ ($p < 0.001$) with the longitudinal weight but $-0.182$ ($p = 0.295$) with the cross-sectional weight (@tbl-b1-main), and the wild cluster bootstrap finds it insignificant under both weights (@tbl-b1-wild and @tbl-b1-lnw). The estimate for low-English owners therefore depends on which few owners receive weight, and we do not treat it as evidence in either direction.


